ZeroBubble paper key facts (Qi et al., ICLR 2024, "Zero Bubble (Almost) Pipeline Parallelism"):
- Standard 1F1B has a bubble of size (pp−1)/(pp + (N−1))... actually bubble ratio for 1F1B is (p−1)/(p + N − 1) where p = number of pipeline stages, N = microbatch count (global, = gradient accumulation * ...). The bubble fraction shrinks with more microbatches.
- ZB splits backward into two parts: Backward-W (weights gradient) and Backward-I (input gradient). This decouples them so they can be scheduled to overlap with communication and fill bubbles.
- ZB achieves near-zero bubble by filling the "warmup/cooldown" bubbles with Backward-W computations.
- Schedules: ZB-H1 (1F1B-like), ZB-H2, ZB-U (unified, generated by search), ZB-V (V-shape), ZB-1p, ZB-2p, etc. The "p" refers to "period" or max-pending-backward. ZB-2p means at most 2 pending backward passes are allowed.
